<!--
  What every project of the new Windows app has in common. MSBuild reads this file
  before each project in this folder or below it, so that these are said once.

  What is NOT here: the names. Each project's assembly and namespace is
  "Pro7ChordEditor." and the project's own name (Pro7ChordEditor.ChordRules,
  Pro7ChordEditor.Song), and it could be said once, here, as that. It is said in each
  project instead, because said here it breaks the building of a window. To build one,
  WPF first builds a copy of the project under a made-up name ("AppViews_x3kq_wpftmp"),
  so that a window can use a view of its own project. A name worked out here from the
  project's name comes out otherwise for the copy, and the window then cannot find the
  view: "The tag 'NotBuiltYet' does not exist in XML namespace ...". (Found, building
  M0.)

  Nullable. A name that may hold nothing has to say so, and code that would use
  nothing where that was not said does not build. It is the one group of warnings made
  into errors here. Not all warnings are: a newer .NET SDK brings new ones, and a build
  that passed yesterday should not stop today only because the tools were updated.
  (The rules about comparing text, which also stop a build, are in .editorconfig.)

  Embedded symbols. What a program needs to say which line an error came from is put
  inside each assembly, so that the app can be given out as one file and still say so
  in its log.

  The version. One number for the whole app, said here once: every assembly has it,
  the app reads its own (AppViews/LibraryModel.cs), and it is written into the index
  of every copy the app keeps of a song (spec/07-backups.md, rule 8), so that a copy
  says which app made it. It is 0.1.0 until there is a build worth giving anyone
  (milestone M5); what it is then, and how it is counted on from there, is the
  maintainer's to say. (The first editor, in old/, was 0.9.5, and is another program.)

  (global.json, beside this file, is what the `dotnet` command itself is told: how
  tests are run.)

  windows/old has a file of this name with nothing in it, which keeps these settings
  from the first editor's project.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);nullable</WarningsAsErrors>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
